  "final_text": "Both objects exist on the Kibana 9.5.5 instance, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-2a763206 Web logs overview\" (id `562f6e85-5725-4953-9bd4-489cc0ce89d9`) with five panels, all on the `kibana_sample_data_logs` data view:\n- Total requests (count)\n- 5xx server errors (count, filtered by KQL `response.keyword : 5*`)\n- Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Bytes transferred (sum of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time (bar chart, date histogram on `@timestamp`)\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-2a763206 5xx spike\" (id `06c2ec76-5091-43ff-9245-77d005bcd357`), an Elasticsearch query rule.\n- Runs every 1 minute.\n- Counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5*` in the last 5 minutes, and fires when the count is above 10.\n- Enabled, with no actions attached. Its last run succeeded and reported 0 alerts.\n\n**Field exploration:** I read the field list and types from the data view. I could not get value counts for `response`, because the console proxy, ES|QL, and the raw search endpoint are all disabled on this instance. Because of that, I used a wildcard for 5xx instead of a fixed list of codes. The first run returned no 5xx hits in the last 5 minutes.\n\n**Not verified:**\n- I did not check the sample data's timestamps. The dashboard uses a last-7-days window, so panels may be empty if the data is older. If so, widen the time range in the dashboard.\n- I did not visually check the dashboard panels.\n\nI added the tag `EFF-2a763206` to the rule so it's easy to find.",
